---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Using the enrolment prototype" actor={Astro.locals.actor}>
  <h1>From finding a course to enrolling</h1>
  <ol class="help-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Create and verify your account.</strong> Use an @anu.edu.au address and a separate prototype
      password. Open the emailed link and confirm, then sign in.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Explore courses.</strong> Search by full code, number, or title. Use the subject filter to
      distinguish codes such as COMP6528 and ENGN6528.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Save courses to consider.</strong> Open a course, choose its offering, and save it to My courses.
      Saved courses are candidates; confirmed enrolments have their own section.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resolve permission requirements.</strong> Read the comparison with your fictional academic
      record and get a demo assessment attached to that offering. Select whether you are relying on the
      record, disputing it, claiming equivalent study or seeking an exception. Your explanation is saved
      as self-reported context; it does not change academic facts. Unknown conditions produce specific
      next steps. Optional staff review is available, but that queue is not staffed automatically.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check your study load.</strong> Confirmed enrolments normally cannot exceed 24 units per half-year.
      A 12-unit course counts twice as much as a 6-unit course; choose a credit value for variable-unit courses.
      If needed, follow Request overload assessment and explain why. The saved report checks the published
      thresholds and identifies missing evidence. Optional program review is separate from course permission.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Confirm enrolment.</strong> Ready courses and approved requests can be confirmed individually.
      It then appears under Confirmed enrolments in My courses and My profile.
      Pending requests do not stop you enrolling in other courses.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Complete example without waiting for staff</h2>
  <p><a href="/demo/">Try the fictional COMP8620 topic scenario</a>. Its separate academic record and topic assumptions demonstrate permission and explicit confirmation. Once confirmed, it appears under Demo scenario completions in My courses and My profile, separate from academic enrolments. All decisions use a demo policy, not ANU authority.</p>
  <h2>Your academic profile</h2>
  <p>Confirmed enrolments now offer <strong>Swap</strong> and <strong>Drop</strong>.
    Drop removes an enrolment from your current load after confirmation. Swap checks
    a replacement in the same session and keeps the original course if it cannot
    complete. Course changes and permission history remain available in My courses.
    These prototype changes do not model official withdrawal deadlines or fees.</p>
  <p>Overload assessment needs exact marks and completed current-program units. Existing demo profiles may lack
    those details, so the report will show what cannot be established. Your message cannot replace academic evidence.
    An overload approval raises the load limit to 30 or 36 units for that half-year; it does not enrol a course.</p>
  <p>
    Your account gets a fictional Master of Computing (Advanced) record with foundation subjects. It is
    demonstration data, not a connection to ANU's student records. View it under My profile.
  </p>
  <h2 id="planning">Suggestions for your next semester</h2>
  <p>New accounts get a versioned Computing (Advanced) scenario with an Artificial Intelligence specialisation,
    completed results for 2026 S1, 2026 S2 and 2027 S1, and a starting plan for 2027 S2. All records and marks are fictional.
    Historical 2026 availability is assumed for the scenario; the saved 2027 rules check its consistency, not real commencement-year requirements.</p>
  <p>My profile suggests courses from your completed results, degree requirements and saved offerings. It prioritises
    remaining requirements and prerequisites for later study, separates permission/evidence cases, and allows for confirmed
    and saved courses when suggesting a load. Current enrolments are not passed credit. Save an option, then confirm enrolment
    through its course page. Review My courses to remove a candidate or drop/swap an enrolment.</p>
  <p>Existing accounts keep their academic history. They can save the AI study focus and planning semester without changing
    their results. Selecting 2028 shows indicative options from the saved 2027 pages; those pages do not open future enrolment.
    Suggestions remain useful if completion takes longer. They do not certify GPA, project approval, credit substitutions or graduation.</p>
  <p>Inside My profile, the Course adviser can match your interests to ready courses and show the saved source excerpts.
    Choose your preferred total units in its separate field; a number in your message does not set that limit.
    Suggestions still pass the normal eligibility and load checks. If AI matching is unavailable, you can still apply
    a unit preference. After changing your semester or courses, update the adviser to use the current record.
    Use standard suggestions returns to the usual planner. The adviser does not approve permissions or enrol you.</p>
  <h2 id="reviewers">For reviewers and the teaching team</h2>
  <p>
    Staff accounts are invitation-only. A reviewer sees requests for their assigned courses, the student's
    explanation, the relevant eligibility checks, and the request history. Approval and rejection are
    recorded; rejection requires a reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    To try reviewer access, arrange an invitation with the project owner using a real @anu.edu.au inbox
    you can receive mail at. Do not register that address as a student first: this version uses separate
    student and reviewer accounts. Open the invitation, choose a prototype password, confirm your email,
    then sign in. Invitations expire after 30 minutes; use Resend verification on the sign-in page if needed.
    If another account is signed in, the invitation offers Sign out and continue. A private browser window
    lets you keep the student session open separately.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your queue lists your assigned courses. To populate it, a student opens one of those courses, chooses
    the request reason, then selects Optional staff review → Send to convenor. Automatic demo assessments
    do not enter the human queue. After reviewer approval, the student confirms enrolment. Course catalogue
    and Course library remain available to reviewers for browsing.
  </p>
  <h2>About the dataset</h2>
  <p>Study-load review uses a separate fictional program reviewer, Dr Avery Hart. The project owner can invite
    an unused reviewer email to that role. This queue receives only overload reports where the student selected
    Send overload request for review. Decisions require an explanation, and a course convenor cannot grant an overload.</p>
  <p>
    Find courses and the course library use the same supplied 2027 pages, including descriptions, learning
    outcomes, requirements and offerings. All 80 course pages have a source-bound interpretation or an
    explicit source gap. Course completions, program restrictions, credit groups and conditional permission
    are checked where the evidence allows. Topic conditions, equivalence, GPA, selection decisions and
    unclear wording can remain unresolved with specific next steps. Missing requisite sections never mean
    automatic eligibility. A course without
    a recorded offering cannot accept an enrolment request. Existing requests retain their original year.
    All reviewer identities and academic records are fictional. The first course-suggestion example covers Computing (Advanced)
    and Artificial Intelligence; other degree and specialisation audits are not implemented.
  </p>
  <h2>Account help</h2>
  <p>To test delivery again after signing in, open My account and choose Send a fresh verification test.
    Confirm receipt through the emailed link. Your existing account, sign-in sessions and saved work stay
    unchanged. You can send up to three tests every 15 minutes; My account shows the latest receipt status.</p>
  <p>
    Verification links last 30 minutes. Request a new one from the sign-in page. Password recovery is not yet
    available; use a separate password you can retain for this prototype.
  </p>
</Layout>
